courses.learning_outcomes:
At the end of the course, students are expected to
1) learn how to collect on-site data for field studies;
2) develop the ability to assess collected data with knowledge from classroom learning;
3) explore and develop their own research topic;
4) compare and contrast research topics across different countries or regions;
5) complete a research report.

After taking this course, students are expected to:
1) Demonstrate comprehensive knowledge of key jurisprudential theories, legal frameworks, statutory provisions, and enforcement practices related to global security and human rights in major geopolitical contexts;
2) Analyze and critically evaluate the relationship between human rights and national security within international relations from a comparative legal perspective;
3) Examine the reciprocal impacts between changes in national security legislation and developments in international relations, recognizing how legal frameworks both shape and respond to geopolitical dynamics; and
4) Evaluate the roles and interactions of domestic institutions (executive, legislature, judiciary) and international organizations in balancing national security concerns with human rights protections.

After taking this course, students are expected to:

* Describe and discuss the goals, structures, functions and operations of an organization in the non-government or public sector;
* Identify the professional skills required in managing such an organization;
* Apply some of the concepts and skills learnt in the programme in a real work setting;
* Put into practice attitudes and personal values in accordance with the requirements of working in the sector;
* Identify own strengths and weaknesses with regard to employment in the sector;
* Prepare for a relevant global exposure in the student exchange program.

After taking this course, students are expected to:

* acquire integrative knowledge and research skills from different disciplines throughout the capstone project;
* apply some of the key concepts and skills learnt on the programme to real-world diplomatic settings;
* use research methods to analyse international and diplomatic issues and provide solutions for global problems.
